1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,493
How One Man Lost $20 Billion in Two Days
In March 2021, a man most people

2
00:00:06,493 --> 00:00:10,617
had never heard of was, on paper, one of
the richest people in the world.

3
00:00:10,777 --> 00:00:14,937
His fortune was reportedly around
twenty billion dollars.

4
00:00:15,337 --> 00:00:17,097
He didn't run a famous company.

5
00:00:17,097 --> 00:00:19,194
He didn't manage a fund you
could invest in.

6
00:00:19,354 --> 00:00:23,674
He traded his own money, quietly,
from an office in New York.

7
00:00:23,914 --> 00:00:25,994
Two days later, the fortune was gone.

8
00:00:25,994 --> 00:00:29,704
And the banks that had lent him money
lost more than ten billion dollars.

9
00:00:29,704 --> 00:00:32,904
So how do you lose twenty
billion dollars that fast?

10
00:00:32,904 --> 00:00:34,824
And who ends up paying for it?

11
00:00:34,824 --> 00:00:36,824
This is Billions and Bulls.

12
00:00:36,824 --> 00:00:38,904
Let's follow the money.

13
00:00:39,813 --> 00:00:41,893
Part 1 The Tiger Cub

14
00:00:41,893 --> 00:00:43,733
His name is Sung Kook Hwang.

15
00:00:43,733 --> 00:00:46,532
On Wall Street, everyone called him Bill.

16
00:00:47,012 --> 00:00:51,012
He learned the business in the 1990s at
Tiger Management, one of the most

17
00:00:51,012 --> 00:00:55,812
famous hedge funds of its time, run by a
legendary investor named Julian

18
00:00:55,812 --> 00:00:56,812
Robertson.

19
00:00:56,796 --> 00:01:00,236
Robertson trained a whole
generation of fund managers.

20
00:01:00,236 --> 00:01:02,796
People called them the Tiger Cubs.

21
00:01:02,796 --> 00:01:04,871
Bill Hwang was one of them.

22
00:01:05,271 --> 00:01:10,551
In 2001, he started his own fund, Tiger
Asia, with Robertson's backing.

23
00:01:10,551 --> 00:01:14,071
It focused on stocks in
China, Japan and Korea.

24
00:01:14,071 --> 00:01:16,826
And for years, it did very well.

25
00:01:17,387 --> 00:01:20,986
Then, in 2012, it ran into
trouble with the law.

26
00:01:20,986 --> 00:01:24,826
The SEC said Hwang and his firm had used
confidential information to bet

27
00:01:24,826 --> 00:01:26,842
against three Chinese bank stocks.

28
00:01:27,002 --> 00:01:31,082
Hwang and his firms agreed to pay
forty-four million dollars to settle.

29
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:33,882
Tiger Asia pleaded guilty to wire fraud.

30
00:01:33,882 --> 00:01:35,168
Hong Kong went further.

31
00:01:35,168 --> 00:01:40,448
In 2014, Hwang was reportedly barred
from trading there for four years.

32
00:01:40,528 --> 00:01:44,048
For most people, that would be the end
of a career managing money.

33
00:01:44,048 --> 00:01:45,501
Hwang found another way.

34
00:01:45,501 --> 00:01:49,741
He closed the fund to outside investors,
and turned it into a family office.

35
00:01:49,741 --> 00:01:52,621
A firm that only manages
one family's money.

36
00:01:52,621 --> 00:01:53,761
His own.

37
00:01:54,241 --> 00:01:56,081
He called it Archegos.

38
00:01:56,081 --> 00:02:00,801
It comes from a Greek word that can mean
"the one who leads the way." And

39
00:02:00,801 --> 00:02:02,588
here's why the structure mattered.

40
00:02:02,748 --> 00:02:07,068
In the United States, a family office
doesn't have to register with regulators

41
00:02:07,068 --> 00:02:09,308
the way a regular investment fund does.

42
00:02:09,308 --> 00:02:11,948
It doesn't have outside
clients to report to.

43
00:02:11,948 --> 00:02:14,317
It can stay almost invisible.

44
00:02:14,477 --> 00:02:19,197
That exemption came from a rule the SEC
wrote in 2011, after the financial

45
00:02:19,197 --> 00:02:20,197
crisis.

46
00:02:20,077 --> 00:02:24,237
The idea was to spare families managing
their own wealth from paperwork

47
00:02:24,237 --> 00:02:25,596
meant for big funds.

48
00:02:25,836 --> 00:02:30,556
Nobody imagined a family office with tens
of billions of dollars in bets.

49
00:02:30,956 --> 00:02:32,666
For a while, that was the whole point.

50
00:02:32,666 --> 00:02:36,026
Archegos could grow without anyone
outside really watching.

51
00:02:36,026 --> 00:02:38,026
And grow it did.

52
00:02:39,066 --> 00:02:41,171
Part 2 The Invisible Bet

53
00:02:41,411 --> 00:02:46,851
According to prosecutors, between March
2020 and March 2021, Hwang's fortune

54
00:02:46,851 --> 00:02:51,504
went from about one and a half billion
dollars to about thirty-five billion.

55
00:02:51,664 --> 00:02:53,824
That's more than twenty times bigger.

56
00:02:53,824 --> 00:02:55,665
In one year.

57
00:02:55,744 --> 00:02:57,344
How do you grow that fast?

58
00:02:57,344 --> 00:02:59,184
Not just by picking good stocks.

59
00:02:59,184 --> 00:03:00,305
By borrowing.

60
00:03:00,305 --> 00:03:01,305
A lot.

61
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:04,843
And the tool Archegos used for
that has a very boring name.

62
00:03:04,843 --> 00:03:07,003
A total return swap.

63
00:03:07,484 --> 00:03:08,843
Here's how it works.

64
00:03:08,843 --> 00:03:12,291
Instead of buying a stock yourself,
you ask a bank to buy it.

65
00:03:12,451 --> 00:03:14,211
The bank owns the shares.

66
00:03:14,211 --> 00:03:16,371
But you get everything the stock does.

67
00:03:16,371 --> 00:03:19,011
If it goes up, the bank pays you the gain.

68
00:03:19,011 --> 00:03:21,653
If it goes down, you pay
the bank the loss.

69
00:03:21,653 --> 00:03:26,213
In return, you pay the bank a fee, and you
put down some money as collateral.

70
00:03:26,213 --> 00:03:28,870
Often just a fraction of the position.

71
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:33,750
So with one dollar of your own, you can
control several dollars of stock.

72
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:35,190
That's called leverage.

73
00:03:35,190 --> 00:03:37,510
And it works in both directions.

74
00:03:37,510 --> 00:03:39,707
Here's a simple example.

75
00:03:39,707 --> 00:03:43,627
Say you put down ten million dollars,
and control fifty million dollars

76
00:03:43,627 --> 00:03:44,627
of stock.

77
00:03:44,507 --> 00:03:48,107
If the stock rises twenty
percent, you make ten million.

78
00:03:48,107 --> 00:03:49,627
You just doubled your money.

79
00:03:49,627 --> 00:03:53,359
If the stock falls twenty
percent, you lose ten million.

80
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:54,719
Everything you put in.

81
00:03:54,719 --> 00:03:55,719
Gone.

82
00:03:55,519 --> 00:03:57,039
Same move in the market.

83
00:03:57,039 --> 00:03:59,839
Five times the effect on you.

84
00:04:00,079 --> 00:04:03,017
But swaps had another
feature Archegos liked.

85
00:04:03,177 --> 00:04:06,857
When an investor buys more than five
percent of a company, they usually

86
00:04:06,857 --> 00:04:08,137
have to tell the public.

87
00:04:08,137 --> 00:04:10,697
With swaps, the bank holds the shares.

88
00:04:10,697 --> 00:04:13,838
So Archegos's name didn't
show up anywhere.

89
00:04:14,317 --> 00:04:16,318
And it didn't use just one bank.

90
00:04:16,318 --> 00:04:17,677
It used several.

91
00:04:17,677 --> 00:04:23,277
Credit Suisse, Nomura, Morgan Stanley,
Goldman Sachs, UBS and others.

92
00:04:23,277 --> 00:04:25,478
Each bank saw only its own piece.

93
00:04:25,638 --> 00:04:28,038
None of them saw the whole picture.

94
00:04:28,438 --> 00:04:32,578
Prosecutors later said that, at its
peak, Archegos controlled about one

95
00:04:32,578 --> 00:04:35,761
hundred and sixty billion
dollars in stock positions.

96
00:04:36,001 --> 00:04:39,281
Built on top of a fortune of
about thirty-five billion.

97
00:04:39,281 --> 00:04:42,481
And much of it was concentrated in
just a handful of companies.

98
00:04:42,481 --> 00:04:44,934
Like ViacomCBS, the media company.

99
00:04:45,094 --> 00:04:48,614
And Chinese companies like
Baidu and Tencent Music.

100
00:04:48,934 --> 00:04:51,174
Prosecutors said something else, too.

101
00:04:51,174 --> 00:04:53,916
That Archegos didn't just
buy these stocks.

102
00:04:54,076 --> 00:04:57,436
It bought them in a way designed to push
their prices up, which made its

103
00:04:57,436 --> 00:05:01,262
own fortune look even bigger, and
let it borrow even more.

104
00:05:01,582 --> 00:05:06,382
Prosecutors also said that Archegos lied
to its banks about how big and

105
00:05:06,382 --> 00:05:09,022
how concentrated its
positions really were.

106
00:05:09,022 --> 00:05:10,142
Hwang denied it.

107
00:05:10,142 --> 00:05:12,337
A jury would later decide.

108
00:05:12,816 --> 00:05:14,896
But first, a question.

109
00:05:14,896 --> 00:05:19,056
Why would the biggest banks in the world
lend so much, to a man with that

110
00:05:19,056 --> 00:05:20,256
history?

111
00:05:21,376 --> 00:05:23,638
Part 3 The Banks That Said Yes

112
00:05:23,638 --> 00:05:26,198
The answer, as usual, is money.

113
00:05:26,198 --> 00:05:29,638
But maybe not as much
money as you'd think.

114
00:05:29,718 --> 00:05:33,049
Banks that serve big traders are
called prime brokers.

115
00:05:33,049 --> 00:05:37,288
They lend money, they hold the trades,
and they earn fees and interest.

116
00:05:37,288 --> 00:05:39,529
A client like Archegos was valuable.

117
00:05:39,529 --> 00:05:42,414
It traded a lot, and it borrowed a lot.

118
00:05:42,893 --> 00:05:46,653
So how much did Credit Suisse
actually earn from Archegos?

119
00:05:46,653 --> 00:05:51,614
In 2020, about seventeen and a half
million dollars in fees.

120
00:05:52,174 --> 00:05:54,219
And how much was Credit Suisse lending?

121
00:05:54,379 --> 00:05:59,579
Its exposure to Archegos reportedly grew to
more than twenty billion dollars.

122
00:05:59,979 --> 00:06:01,899
Keep that fee number in mind.

123
00:06:01,899 --> 00:06:03,564
Seventeen and a half million.

124
00:06:03,804 --> 00:06:07,244
Because a few months later, Credit Suisse
would lose about five and a half

125
00:06:07,244 --> 00:06:09,404
billion dollars on the same client.

126
00:06:09,404 --> 00:06:13,484
That's more than three hundred times what
it earned from Archegos in a whole

127
00:06:13,484 --> 00:06:14,684
year.

128
00:06:14,684 --> 00:06:15,684
Quick thing.

129
00:06:15,448 --> 00:06:18,248
If you like videos that follow
the money, subscribe.

130
00:06:18,248 --> 00:06:19,248
It's free.

131
00:06:19,128 --> 00:06:21,448
And it comes with zero leverage.

132
00:06:21,928 --> 00:06:25,768
After the collapse, Credit Suisse hired a
law firm to investigate what went

133
00:06:25,768 --> 00:06:26,768
wrong.

134
00:06:26,521 --> 00:06:28,121
The report was brutal.

135
00:06:28,121 --> 00:06:32,601
It described a "fundamental failure of
management and controls" in the investment

136
00:06:32,601 --> 00:06:33,601
bank.

137
00:06:33,241 --> 00:06:36,600
Archegos had broken its risk
limits again and again.

138
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:39,000
People inside the bank had
raised the alarm.

139
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,640
And the bank kept lending anyway.

140
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,600
To be fair to the banks,
there's another side.

141
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:48,246
According to prosecutors, Archegos
misled them on purpose.

142
00:06:48,246 --> 00:06:52,325
Some banks were told that Archegos's
positions were smaller and more spread

143
00:06:52,325 --> 00:06:54,485
out than they really were.

144
00:06:54,726 --> 00:06:56,005
And the regulators?

145
00:06:56,005 --> 00:07:00,005
Archegos wasn't registered as an
investment adviser, and its swaps weren't

146
00:07:00,005 --> 00:07:01,005
public.

147
00:07:00,724 --> 00:07:03,524
Nobody outside had the full view.

148
00:07:03,764 --> 00:07:05,893
Still, it's hard to ignore the math.

149
00:07:06,053 --> 00:07:09,973
The banks were taking a risk measured in
billions, to earn fees measured

150
00:07:09,973 --> 00:07:10,973
in millions.

151
00:07:10,933 --> 00:07:15,013
As long as the stocks kept going up,
it looked like free money.

152
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:17,817
And then one of them went down.

153
00:07:18,697 --> 00:07:21,257
Part 4 Two Days in March

154
00:07:21,257 --> 00:07:27,417
On Monday, March 22, 2021, ViacomCBS
stock closed at a record high, just

155
00:07:27,417 --> 00:07:29,516
above one hundred dollars a share.

156
00:07:29,516 --> 00:07:32,237
It had more than doubled since
the start of the year.

157
00:07:32,237 --> 00:07:36,396
Investors were excited about streaming,
and ViacomCBS had just launched

158
00:07:36,396 --> 00:07:38,619
its own service, Paramount Plus.

159
00:07:38,779 --> 00:07:42,619
A big part of that rise, it would later
turn out, came from Archegos's own

160
00:07:42,619 --> 00:07:43,819
buying.

161
00:07:44,219 --> 00:07:48,699
That evening, ViacomCBS announced it would
sell new shares, to raise billions

162
00:07:48,699 --> 00:07:49,699
of dollars.

163
00:07:49,468 --> 00:07:51,067
It made sense for the company.

164
00:07:51,067 --> 00:07:54,348
Its stock was expensive, so it
was a good time to sell.

165
00:07:54,348 --> 00:07:55,948
But the market didn't like it.

166
00:07:55,948 --> 00:07:59,627
By Wednesday, the deal was priced at
eighty-five dollars a share.

167
00:07:59,627 --> 00:08:02,267
About fifteen percent below
Monday's record.

168
00:08:02,267 --> 00:08:04,828
And the stock dropped with it.

169
00:08:04,828 --> 00:08:07,359
For a normal investor, that's a bad week.

170
00:08:07,359 --> 00:08:09,919
For Archegos, it was a disaster.

171
00:08:09,919 --> 00:08:11,279
Remember the leverage.

172
00:08:11,279 --> 00:08:14,479
When the stock fell, the banks
asked for more collateral.

173
00:08:14,479 --> 00:08:15,919
It's called a margin call.

174
00:08:15,919 --> 00:08:18,338
And Archegos didn't have the cash.

175
00:08:18,658 --> 00:08:22,978
On Thursday, the banks reportedly met with
Archegos, to talk about selling

176
00:08:22,978 --> 00:08:26,578
the stocks slowly, together, so they
wouldn't crash the market.

177
00:08:26,578 --> 00:08:28,040
They couldn't agree.

178
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,760
It's a classic trap.

179
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:34,200
If every bank sells slowly,
everyone loses a little.

180
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:38,680
But if one bank sells first, it gets the
best prices, and everyone else

181
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:39,680
gets what's left.

182
00:08:39,839 --> 00:08:42,079
So nobody wants to wait.

183
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:44,640
So, on Friday, it became a race.

184
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:46,959
Each bank wanted to sell
before the others.

185
00:08:46,959 --> 00:08:49,365
Huge blocks of stock hit
the market at once.

186
00:08:49,525 --> 00:08:50,805
ViacomCBS.

187
00:08:50,805 --> 00:08:51,805
Discovery.

188
00:08:51,605 --> 00:08:52,605
Baidu.

189
00:08:52,405 --> 00:08:53,765
Tencent Music.

190
00:08:53,765 --> 00:08:58,245
By the end of that Friday, ViacomCBS had
fallen to about forty-eight dollars.

191
00:08:58,245 --> 00:09:00,822
Less than half of where it was on Monday.

192
00:09:01,302 --> 00:09:05,382
Prosecutors later said that more than one
hundred billion dollars in market

193
00:09:05,382 --> 00:09:09,029
value disappeared from nearly a
dozen companies, within days.

194
00:09:09,188 --> 00:09:13,268
And Bill Hwang's own fortune, reported at
around twenty billion dollars,

195
00:09:13,268 --> 00:09:16,068
was gone in about two days.

196
00:09:16,469 --> 00:09:18,228
But here's the thing about a race.

197
00:09:18,228 --> 00:09:20,438
Somebody finishes last.

198
00:09:21,638 --> 00:09:23,558
Part 5 Who Paid

199
00:09:23,558 --> 00:09:26,118
The banks that sold first did best.

200
00:09:26,118 --> 00:09:30,535
Goldman Sachs moved early, and
reportedly came out almost unscathed.

201
00:09:30,694 --> 00:09:35,414
The banks that hesitated were left
holding stocks that were falling fast.

202
00:09:35,814 --> 00:09:39,255
Credit Suisse lost about five and
a half billion dollars.

203
00:09:39,255 --> 00:09:40,628
The biggest loss of all.

204
00:09:40,788 --> 00:09:44,388
That's about twice what the whole bank
had earned in profit in 2020.

205
00:09:44,388 --> 00:09:49,348
Nomura, Japan's biggest investment bank,
lost about two point nine billion.

206
00:09:49,348 --> 00:09:52,080
Morgan Stanley lost about
nine hundred million.

207
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:54,880
UBS, about eight hundred
and sixty million.

208
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:58,880
Mitsubishi UFJ, about two
hundred and seventy million.

209
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:02,747
Add it up, and the banks lost more
than ten billion dollars.

210
00:10:02,987 --> 00:10:06,667
On one family office most
people had never heard of.

211
00:10:06,667 --> 00:10:09,467
At Credit Suisse, the damage
didn't stop there.

212
00:10:09,467 --> 00:10:12,641
Its head of investment banking and
its chief risk officer left.

213
00:10:12,801 --> 00:10:16,561
The bank cut its dividend, and
reportedly had to raise new capital from

214
00:10:16,561 --> 00:10:17,561
investors.

215
00:10:17,441 --> 00:10:21,041
The loss became one more crack in
the bank's reputation.

216
00:10:21,041 --> 00:10:23,878
Two years later, Credit
Suisse itself collapsed.

217
00:10:23,878 --> 00:10:27,318
That's the story we told in our
Credit Suisse episode.

218
00:10:27,638 --> 00:10:29,398
Not everyone lost, though.

219
00:10:29,398 --> 00:10:32,037
ViacomCBS itself did just fine.

220
00:10:32,037 --> 00:10:36,357
It had sold its new shares at eighty-five
dollars, raising billions, days

221
00:10:36,357 --> 00:10:38,117
before the crash.

222
00:10:38,517 --> 00:10:40,976
But there were other people
on the losing side.

223
00:10:41,216 --> 00:10:45,296
Investors who bought ViacomCBS and the
other stocks near the top, without

224
00:10:45,296 --> 00:10:48,656
knowing that a single hidden buyer
had pushed the prices up.

225
00:10:48,656 --> 00:10:50,736
And Archegos's own employees.

226
00:10:50,736 --> 00:10:52,681
Some had money tied up in the firm.

227
00:10:52,681 --> 00:10:55,001
They lost it too.

228
00:10:55,241 --> 00:10:58,601
So who paid for Bill Hwang's
twenty billion dollars?

229
00:10:58,601 --> 00:11:00,761
Mostly, the money was never really there.

230
00:11:00,761 --> 00:11:01,766
It was borrowed.

231
00:11:01,926 --> 00:11:05,126
When it disappeared, the banks that
had lent it paid the bill.

232
00:11:05,126 --> 00:11:07,526
And through them, their shareholders.

233
00:11:09,126 --> 00:11:12,328
Part 6 The Verdict, and the
Rule That Never Came

234
00:11:12,568 --> 00:11:17,289
In April 2022, Bill Hwang was arrested
and charged with fraud and market

235
00:11:17,289 --> 00:11:18,328
manipulation.

236
00:11:18,328 --> 00:11:22,808
The Justice Department, the SEC and the
CFTC all brought cases at the same

237
00:11:22,808 --> 00:11:23,808
time.

238
00:11:23,532 --> 00:11:28,164
Two former Archegos executives pleaded
guilty, and testified against him.

239
00:11:28,564 --> 00:11:34,644
In July 2024, a jury convicted Hwang on ten
criminal counts, including securities

240
00:11:34,644 --> 00:11:37,722
fraud, wire fraud and market manipulation.

241
00:11:37,722 --> 00:11:42,122
The firm's chief financial officer,
Patrick Halligan, was convicted at the

242
00:11:42,122 --> 00:11:43,122
same trial.

243
00:11:43,081 --> 00:11:45,112
He later received eight years.

244
00:11:45,272 --> 00:11:50,152
In November 2024, a judge sentenced
Hwang to eighteen years in prison.

245
00:11:50,152 --> 00:11:53,912
He was also ordered to pay more than nine
billion dollars in restitution

246
00:11:53,912 --> 00:11:56,854
to seven banks and a group
of former employees.

247
00:11:57,013 --> 00:11:59,493
His lawyers said he would appeal.

248
00:11:59,973 --> 00:12:01,893
But what about the hidden part?

249
00:12:01,893 --> 00:12:05,814
The swaps that let one man control one
hundred and sixty billion dollars

250
00:12:05,814 --> 00:12:08,437
of stock, without the public knowing?

251
00:12:08,917 --> 00:12:12,357
In December 2021, the SEC proposed a rule.

252
00:12:12,357 --> 00:12:15,878
Anyone holding very large positions
through swaps would have to report them

253
00:12:15,878 --> 00:12:16,878
publicly.

254
00:12:16,677 --> 00:12:18,121
The industry pushed back.

255
00:12:18,121 --> 00:12:22,680
It argued that regulators already receive
swap data, and that public disclosure

256
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:24,440
could hurt investors.

257
00:12:24,440 --> 00:12:28,074
In June 2025, the SEC
withdrew the proposal.

258
00:12:28,635 --> 00:12:33,114
So, today, regulators do see much
more swap data than before.

259
00:12:33,114 --> 00:12:36,892
But the public still can't see a bet
like Archegos building up.

260
00:12:37,452 --> 00:12:41,452
So, how does one man lose twenty
billion dollars in two days?

261
00:12:41,692 --> 00:12:43,052
He borrows most of it.

262
00:12:43,052 --> 00:12:45,932
He hides it in swaps, spread
across many banks.

263
00:12:45,932 --> 00:12:47,854
And he puts it into a few stocks.

264
00:12:47,854 --> 00:12:50,494
On the way up, leverage looks like genius.

265
00:12:50,494 --> 00:12:53,774
On the way down, it moves just as fast.

266
00:12:54,014 --> 00:12:55,854
Leverage doesn't create money.

267
00:12:55,854 --> 00:12:57,589
It only borrows it from the future.

268
00:12:57,749 --> 00:13:01,509
And sometimes, the future calls
the loan in two days.

269
00:13:01,829 --> 00:13:02,869
Remember the name?

270
00:13:02,869 --> 00:13:03,869
Archegos.

271
00:13:03,829 --> 00:13:05,349
The one who leads the way.

272
00:13:05,349 --> 00:13:07,266
In the end, it did lead the way.

273
00:13:07,506 --> 00:13:12,066
Into one of the biggest losses Wall
Street's banks had seen in years.

274
